<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fondaco dei Turchi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fontego dei Turchi, Turks' Inn, Türk Hanı, Palace of the Dukes of Ferrara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fondaco dei Turchi is a palazzo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> whose façade combines Islamic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and Byzantine elements. Built as a private palace, it was acquired by the Republic of Venice in 1381 and given to Nicolò II d'Este, and for over two centuries it housed the ambassadors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. After its sale to the senator Antonio Priuli in 1602 it became a fondaco, a combined home, warehouse and market for the Ottoman merchants the Venetians called Turks, who lived there under set hours and trading rules until 1838. The government mostly rebuilt it in 1869, keeping only the canal façade, and it now houses Venice's Natural History Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-turchi.jpg" alt="Fondaco dei Turchi" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Süßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_di_storia_naturale_(Venice)-msu-4997-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ottoman merchants stayed at the <b>Fondaco dei Turchi</b>, which held a small mosque, but did not settle.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li></ul></section>
